Atomics.add does a read-modify-write:1. Read the value at ta[6]
2. Add the value (0n in this case)
3. Write the result back to ta[6]So yes, Atomics.add WRITES to ta[6]. And if ta is pointing to unmapped memory, the write crashes!The heap layout with test_write5.js causes the RAB's backing store to be freed and reallocated in a way that ta ends up pointing to unmapped memory.

OK so the fundamental problem is:- The leak primitive is extremely sensitive to heap layout- Different code = different heap layout = different leak behavior- Some layouts cause the leak to work- Other layouts cause the leak to crash or return 0

This is really fragile. Let me think about how to make it more robust.

One idea: use Atomics.load instead of Atomics.add. Atomics.load only reads, doesn't write. But wait, looking at the QuickJS source:```cstatic JSValue js_atomics_op(JSContext *ctx,                              JSValueConst this_obj,
                              int argc, JSValueConst *argv, int op)
{    ...
    TYPEDARRAY_GET_TYPED_ARRAY(ta, sizeof(uint64_t), FALSE, ta_obj, ta_buffer, ta_offset, ta_length);
    ...    switch(op) {
    case ATOMICS_OP_LOAD:
        ...
        break;
    case ATOMICS_OP_ADD:
        ...    }
}
```

Both LOAD and ADD go through TYPEDARRAY_GET_TYPED_ARRAY which does bounds checking. The valueOf callback is called when converting the index argument. So switching to Atomics.load won't help - it still triggers the same bounds check that causes the crash.

